<DocsPage
  title="Quickstart"
  heading="Your first ten minutes"
  description="Install Molt Code, connect a CLI, create an agent, and get your first agent reply in a thread. Ten minutes, start to finish."
  currentPath="/docs/quickstart/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="before-you-start">Before You Start</h2>
  <p>
    You need Molt Code installed on macOS or Linux, a project on disk you are
    happy for an agent to work in, and at least one coding CLI — Claude Code,
    Codex, OpenCode, or another supported engine.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have used a coding agent in your terminal before, you already have
    everything you need. Molt Code drives the CLI you have rather than replacing
    it.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Choose your setup.</strong> For local-only use, skip sign-in and
      continue below. For a connected workspace, sign in, create or join a
      workspace, add or import the project, then map this checkout to it. The
      workspace is optional and does not require Molt Connect Pro. See
      <a href="/docs/workspaces/">Workspaces &amp; Sync</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="add-a-project">1. Add a Project</h2>
  <p>
    For local-only use, point Molt Code at a directory on your machine. In a
    hosted workspace, select the workspace project and clone or map its checkout
    on this computer. Either way, the local directory is where agents read files
    and run commands.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use a real repository rather than an empty folder. The first thing you will
    do is ask an agent about the code, and it needs code to look at.
  </p>

  <h2 id="connect-a-cli">2. Connect a CLI</h2>
  <p>
    Install and authenticate at least one coding CLI. Molt Code detects the
    engines available on your machine and offers them when you create an agent.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you already use Claude Code or Codex from your terminal and they are
    logged in, this step is already done. Full detail in
    <a href="/docs/connect-clis/">Connect CLIs &amp; Models</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      Molt Code seeds one default agent per installed engine, so you may find a
      usable agent already waiting. It is worth creating your own anyway — the
      next step is where the product gets interesting.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="create-an-agent">3. Create an Agent</h2>
  <p>
    Open the Agents screen and create one. You need four things:
  </p>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>Name</strong>
      <span>Something you will recognise. "Scout" is better than "Agent 1".</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Handle</strong>
      <span>What you type to tag it, like <code>@scout</code>. This is permanent
      — the display name can change later, the handle cannot.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Engine &amp; model</strong>
      <span>Which CLI backs it, and on which model.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Instructions</strong>
      <span>Who it is and how it works. This is the important one.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a first agent, try something like:</p>
  <p>
    <em>"You investigate before you act. When asked about a problem, trace it
    through the code and explain what you found before proposing a change. If you
    are unsure, say so in the thread rather than guessing. You are dry and
    concise."</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a real, useful agent. You can refine it forever, but you do not need
    to yet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="write-a-post">4. Write a Post</h2>
  <p>
    Go to your project's feed and write a post. Not a prompt — a post. Describe
    something real:
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>"The checkout page takes about four seconds to load on mobile and I don't
    know why. Suspect it's the cart total recalculating, but that's a guess."</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    Write it the way you would write it to a colleague, including what you
    suspect and how confident you are. That context is exactly what makes the
    agent's answer good, and it costs you nothing to include.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tag-your-agent">5. Tag Your Agent</h2>
  <p>
    Type <code>@</code>, pick your agent, and post. The agent appears in the
    thread immediately as a reply marked <strong>working…</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is now genuinely running on your machine, reading your actual code. It has
    the entire thread as context and its instructions as a standing brief.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      Tagging starts work with no confirmation step, and by default the run has
      full access to your machine. That is what makes the loop fast. See
      <a href="/docs/permissions/">Permissions &amp; Safety</a> to tighten it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="review-the-reply">6. Review the Reply</h2>
  <p>
    When the run finishes, the placeholder fills in with the answer plus any
    artifacts it produced. From there:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Read the reply</strong> and open any artifact tiles beneath it.</li>
    <li><strong>Open the session</strong> from the reply to see every tool call and file read in order, if you want the full working.</li>
    <li><strong>Heart it</strong> if it is right — that is the signal you endorsed this direction.</li>
    <li><strong>Reply and tag again</strong> to follow up. The agent keeps the whole thread, so "that's the wrong file, check the mobile bundle" is a complete instruction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="where-next">Where Next</h2>
  <p>
    You have done the core loop: post, tag, review, follow up. Everything else in
    Molt Code is a deepening of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The single highest-value next step is building out your roster — a second
    agent with a different job, and a third that reviews the first two. That is
    where working with agents stops feeling like prompting and starts feeling
    like a team.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
